content:
The convenience of `IReactorClient` costs you error fidelity. On a `FAILED` job the client rethrows the message and nothing else:

```typescript
// inside ReactorClient, after the job reaches a terminal status
if (job.status === JobStatus.FAILED) {
  throw new Error(job.error?.message);
}
```

The same pattern runs in `execute`, `executeBatch`, `create`, `deleteDocument`, `deleteDocuments`, `addRelationship`, and `removeRelationship`. Two consequences:

- The thrown `Error.message` equals `job.error?.message`, which **can be `undefined`** if `error` was never set. Guard before displaying it.
- The original error class, stack, and `errorHistory` are gone. You cannot `instanceof` your way to a diagnosis at this boundary.

To recover the detail, drop to the lower-level `IReactor.getJobStatus(jobId)` and read the `JobInfo`:

```typescript
type JobInfo = {
  id: string; documentId: string; status: JobStatus;
  error?: { message: string; stack: string };        // the failing error
  errorHistory?: Array<{ message: string; stack: string }>; // one per attempt, oldest first
  job?: Job;                                          // populated on failure for debugging
  consistencyToken: ConsistencyToken; meta: JobMeta;
  // ...
};
```

`errorHistory` is the payoff: a job the queue retried carries **every** failure, with `error` being the most recent. That is invisible from the client boundary, where you see one message. The `{ message, stack }` shape is structural — treat it as a type you match, not one you import; the barrel's exported `ErrorInfo` is a different (worker-protocol) type aliased as `WorkerErrorInfo`.

Several internal write-path errors lose their typed identity at this edge and survive only as a message string: `DocumentDeletedError`, `DocumentNotFoundError`, `CreateDocumentRequiredError`, `InvalidSignatureError`, and `UpgradeManifestNotFoundError`. None are exported, so branching on them means matching `err.message` — brittle by construction.

Related edge: `ReactorClient.deleteDocument` throws a plain `new Error("Operation aborted")` rather than an `AbortError` when its signal fires during cascade traversal, so prefer handling that call without relying on `err.name === "AbortError"`.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/08-ErrorHandling.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
